    "If an engine uses a naive absolute difference calculation where candidate byte values must match baseline byte values identically, virtually every single visual regression run across different continuous integration workers will fail. Modern graphics processing units and operating system rasterizers do not render geometric vector shapes such as rounded corner borders or TrueType font glyphs as binary black-and-white grids. Instead, they perform subpixel anti-aliasing. When a diagonal curve intersects a square pixel, the rasterizer colors the pixel with an intermediate blend of the foreground and background colors to fool the human eye into perceiving a perfectly smooth line rather than a jagged staircase.",
    "The exact blend of intermediate colors produced during anti-aliasing depends on subtle system configurations: the subpixel layout of the physical display (RGB versus BGR stripe arrangement), the operating system's font rendering engine (FreeType on Linux, CoreText on macOS, DirectWrite on Windows), and the graphics rendering backend (ANGLE, SwiftShader, or native OpenGL). Consequently, the pixels along the boundary edges of text glyphs, icons, and rounded border radii will almost always vary by five to twenty intensity levels between development workstations and Linux CI runners.",
