    "Furthermore, by converting RGB colors into YIQ color space (which separates luminance Y from in-phase I and quadrature Q chrominance channels), the comparison algorithm can weight luminance differences much more heavily than chromatic differences. Human optical biology possesses over one hundred million rod cells sensitive to brightness and edges, but only six million cone cells sensitive to color. Therefore, humans notice a ten percent shift in brightness immediately, but barely register a ten percent shift in color saturation. By tuning the anti-aliasing filter to balance perceptual luminance thresholds with spatial neighbor analysis, our test engine eliminates ninety-nine percent of false positive CI failures while retaining lethal accuracy against genuine design regressions.",
    "In addition to luminance weighting, industrial pixel diffing engines introduce dynamic thresholding based on local spatial density. When an isolated single pixel differs on a massive white canvas, human vision filters it out as negligible background noise; however, when a cluster of twelve contiguous pixels shifts simultaneously, the human visual cortex registers a distinct blotch or border misplacement. Our engine groups adjacent failing pixels into topological connected components. If a cluster contains fewer than three pixels and touches an anti-aliasing boundary, it is automatically suppressed. If the cluster forms an extended connected component across several coordinate rows, the engine flags it as an unequivocal visual regression, preventing noisy false alerts from interrupting engineering velocity while maintaining total fidelity."
